## Why This Is Asked

Tests handling of data sovereignty, per-tenant policies, auditability, and recovery in a real-world LLM-ops gateway.

## Key Concepts

- Data locality and regional routing
- Per-tenant model versioning
- Configurable data retention and automatic purge
- Tamper-evident, cryptographically signed logs
- Deterministic replay for audits

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: sign and log
function logAudit(entry, key) {
  const payload = JSON.stringify(entry);
  const sig = HMACSHA256(key, payload);
  saveLog({payload, sig, ts: Date.now()});
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test purge operations without impacting live traffic?
- How would you handle cross-region latency spikes while preserving policy guarantees?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of policy-driven routing and privacy in a beginner-friendly way, focusing on practical data flow.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped policies
- Ingress routing with model selection
- Per-tenant redaction rules
- Observability and audit trails

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: route based on tenant policy
function route(req, policyStore){
  const p = policyStore.get(req.tenantId);
  if(!p) throw new Error('no policy');
  const m = p.allowedModels.includes(req.dataSensitivity) ? 'ModelA' : 'ModelB';
  // apply redaction if needed, then forward to m
  return m;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test latency guarantees across tenants?
- How would you handle policy drift when tenants update their settings?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end design of a multi-tenant LLM-ops pipeline balancing latency, privacy, policy management, and observability. It assesses ability to design streaming prompts, per-tenant models, hot-updatable policies, drift alerts, and tests.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy engine
- Data locality and privacy controls
- Streaming prompt handling
- Dynamic model routing
- Drift detection and audits
- Tamper-evident logs and auditable trails

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: pick a model based on policy
function selectModel(tenant, policy) {
  const p = policy[tenant] || {};
  if (p.forceGuard) return 'safety-guarded';
  if (p.latencyBudget && p.latencyBudget < 75) return 'base';
  return p.preferredModel ?? 'base';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate drift triggers in production without constant retraining?
- What would be your approach to simulate hot policy updates during peak load?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical fault-tolerance thinking in multi-tenant LLM ops, focusing on isolation, degradation, and testability.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant SLAs; circuit breakers; graceful degradation
- Fallback models and caching; backoff strategies
- Incident testing with staged outages

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch of a simple per-tenant circuit breaker
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(limit=5, timeoutMs=1000){ this.failures=0; this.state='CLOSED'; this.limit=limit; this.timeoutMs=timeoutMs; }
  recordFailure(){ this.failures++; if(this.failures>=this.limit) this.state='OPEN'; }
  reset(){ this.failures=0; this.state='CLOSED'; }
  canProceed(){ return this.state!=='OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate outages in a CI pipeline?
- How would you dimension fallbacks for tenants with strict latency budgets?

